The 30-second interview answer
“I would use Array.from({length: m}, () => new Array(n).fill(0)). The callback runs per row, so each row is a separate array. The tempting alternative, Array(m).fill(Array(n).fill(0)), is broken because fill copies one row reference into every slot, so writing to one cell writes to every row. The inner fill(0) is safe because zero is a primitive. And if the matrix is very large, I would consider a flat typed array with index math instead."
That answer gives working code, names the trap, explains why the trap happens (reference vs value), and shows awareness of performance at scale. That is the difference between memorizing a snippet and understanding the language.
